from pathlib import Path
import pandas as pd,json,hashlib
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;O=R/'results';s=pd.read_csv(O/'summary.csv');d=pd.read_csv(O/'deltas.csv')
def table(z):return z.round(3).to_html(index=False)
q=s[(s.cohort=='all')&(s.dimension=='overall')&(s.annotator=='majority')].copy()
for k in ['f1','precision','recall','accuracy','nn_rate']:q[k]*=100
sens=d[(d.cohort=='all')&(d.dimension=='overall')&(d.policy.isin(['fixed50','anchor_average']))].copy()
for k in ['f1','precision','recall','accuracy']:sens[k]*=100
j=d[(d.cohort=='all')&(d.dimension=='model')&(d.annotator=='majority')&(d.policy=='fixed50')].copy()
for k in ['f1','precision','recall','accuracy']:j[k]*=100
html='''<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Suppressing contrasting neighbours</title><style>body{font:17px system-ui;max-width:1150px;margin:40px auto;padding:0 20px;line-height:1.5;color:#172435}table{border-collapse:collapse;font-size:14px}td,th{padding:8px;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd}th{background:#edf3f8}h2{margin-top:35px}.note{background:#edf3f8;padding:20px;border-radius:10px}</style></head><body><h1>What happens if we never show contrast-labelled neighbours?</h1><p>When important contrast is YES, substitute the original no-explanation prediction. Otherwise retain the original NN prediction. Cases labelled both similarity and contrast YES are suppressed too. Uncertain, missing or unresolved contrast labels retain NN. No examples are removed, no thresholds are refitted, and no new model calls are made.</p><div class="note"><b>Small, inconsistent benefit:</b> majority-label suppression changes macro test F1 from 79.187% to 79.297% at fixed50 (+0.110 points), while accuracy drops 0.167 points. With the three shared tuned threshold settings averaged, F1 changes from 85.322% to 85.509% (+0.188 points). At fixed50, 227 combinations improve, 249 worsen and 24 tie. This is a post-hoc rule assessed on a previously inspected test set, not fresh confirmation.</div><h2>Majority-label results</h2><p>Absolute metrics are percentages, averaged equally across the same 500 original judge/backbone/dataset combinations. Each method receives the same probe/judge thresholds and test support. Local full-population estimates and API matched-valid estimates follow the prior core analysis. Direct judge50 is a separate no-probe-gate sensitivity.</p>'''+table(q[['policy','method','f1','precision','recall','accuracy','nn_rate']])+'''<h2>Dependence on the model that labels contrast</h2><p>Each row uses that annotation model's labels to gate all ten original outcome judges. These are differences from always-NN, in percentage points. The annotator is not necessarily the judge whose classification is evaluated.</p>'''+table(sens[['policy','annotator','f1','precision','recall','accuracy','wins','losses','ties']])+'''<h2>Fixed50 majority gate, by original outcome judge</h2>'''+table(j[['group','f1','precision','recall','accuracy','wins','losses','ties']])+'''<h2>Implication for an include-explanation classifier</h2><p>The semantic contrast label is not the same as expected usefulness. We have both control and NN outputs on validation cases, so can construct supervision directly: prefer NN where it corrects control, prefer control where NN introduces an error, and treat unchanged predictions as indifferent. A learned selector should use only information available before the final judge call. Fit/tune it using separate portions or cross-validation within validation, and evaluate the frozen policy on test. Mechanism labels could be optional input features, but new validation annotations would then be required for those features.</p><p>This experiment tests an existing annotation gate; it does not establish that a new learned selector will improve performance. Small F1 gains can coexist with reduced accuracy because the rule changes the precision/recall balance.</p><h2>Checks and downloads</h2><p>All 5,000 original control/always-NN metric rows match the preceding checked analysis. Source scores, design and annotation inputs are checksummed. Output coverage includes every originally eligible paired test case; missing API predictions are treated exactly as before.</p><ul>'''+''.join('<li><a href="'+x+'">'+x+'</a></li>' for x in ['cell_metrics.csv','summary.csv','deltas.csv','changed_outcomes.csv','COMPLETED.json'])+'</ul></body></html>'
(O/'report.html').write_text(html)
receipt={'files':{p.name:hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest() for p in O.iterdir() if p.is_file() and p.name!='VERIFIED.json'},'check':'5000 control/always-NN metric replays passed; identical thresholds and paired support; no new inference.'};(O/'VERIFIED.json').write_text(json.dumps(receipt,indent=2))
print(q[q.policy.isin(['fixed50','anchor_average'])][['policy','method','f1','precision','recall','accuracy']].to_string(index=False))
